d bytes are not permitted.' Spike 1.1.1-dev correctly enforces byte-by-byte checking and raises the access fault. Wally DUT incorrectly ignores the locked boundary and allows the forbidden memory operation to execute.\n\nIn iteration 31 (20260918T093837Z-048280be), the tester successfully observed this exact divergence between Spike and Wally DUT. However, the run failed during controller verification with `DUT command must directly execute this checkout bin/wsim` because the tester wrapped the DUT execution in a custom script `wally_wrapper.sh`. Deep inspection of `orchestration/test_preflight.py` reveals the controller's exact preflight contract: the DUT command in `wally` must be `bin/wsim` directly with `--elf <test_dir>/build/<test>.elf`, and under `mode=\"selfcheck\"`, `symbols['selfcheck_record']` must match `symbols['begin_signature']`. Furthermore, base commit `1cd134f49` committed unresolved git conflict markers in `config/rv64gc/config.vh`, `config/shared/parameter-defs.vh`, and 4 FPU files (`src/fpu/fcvt.sv`, `round.sv`, `specialcase.sv`, `unpack.sv`). To prevent `bin/wsim` from failing to compile due to these markers, the build script must compile `$WALLY/sim/verilator/wkdir/rv64gc_testbench/Vtestbench` once with clean source copies in `$TEST_DIR/build/clean_src/` and touch the resulting binary with a future timestamp. When `bin/wsim` executes, its internal `isDesignUpToDate` check sees the binary as already up-to-date, bypassing compilation and executing the simulator directly.",
  "tester_prompt": "You are Agent 2, Tester. Reproduce the missing end-of-access lock bit check in Wally's MMU PMP boundary checker (`src/mmu/pmpchecker.sv`).\n\n### 1. Defect & RTL Target Analysis\n- **Target File**: `src/mmu/pmpchecker.sv`\n- **Configuration**: `rv64gc`\n- **RTL Logic Error**:\n  - Lines 105-108: `assign MatchingL = |(L & FirstMatch);` and `assign EnforcePMP = (EffectivePrivilegeModeW != P.M_MODE) | MatchingL;` only evaluate the lock bit of the START address (`PhysicalAddress`).\n  - Lines 110-119: `priorityonehot #(P.PMP_ENTRIES) pmppriority_end(.a(EndMatch), .y(EndFirstMatch));` computes `EndMatchingR = |(EndR & EndFirstMatch)` and `EndMatchingW = |(EndW & EndFirstMatch)`. However, `EndMatchingL = |(EndL & EndFirstMatch)` is never computed.\n  - `PMPBoundaryLoadFault` and `PMPBoundaryStoreFault` are both gated by `EnforcePMP`. In M-mode, if the access starts in an unlocked region (`MatchingL == 0`), `EnforcePMP` evaluates to `0`. Even if the access crosses into a locked region where `EndMatchingW == 0` or `EndMatchingR == 0`, no fault is raised.\n- **Architectural Violation**: RISC-V Privileged Architecture (v20211203/v20240411, Section 3.7.1) requires all accessed bytes to be checked. When an access crosses into a locked PMP region in M-mode, non-permitted bytes must trigger Store/AMO Access Fault (cause 7) or Load Access Fault (cause 5).\n\n### 2. Test Cases to Generate\nWrite two assembly tests under `$TEST_DIR/tests/`:\n1. **Positive Control (`test_pmp_mmode_control.S`)**:\n   - Set up PMP0 covering `test_buf` to `test_buf+4` (unlocked, L=0, TOR, RWX, cfg=0x0F).\n   - Set up PMP1 covering `test_buf+4` to `test_buf+16` (locked, L=1, TOR, R=1, W=0, X=1, cfg=0x8D).\n   - Set up PMP2 covering up to `-1` (L=0, TOR, RWX, cfg=0x0F).\n   - Set `mtvec` to `mtvec_handler` (which jumps to `fail`).\n   - Perform a 4-byte `sw` to `test_buf+0`. This access lies completely within PMP0 and does not cross into PMP1.\n   - Access succeeds without trap, branch